 From the definition of an expectation we gave we can compute 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(x\right)\right\rangle =\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle =0\times0.2+1\times0.5+4\times0.3=1.7,\]

\end_inset 

where we have dropped the subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x\right)$
\end_inset 

, as we consider it clear from the context what the expectation is considered
 to be over.
 We can also see that the expectation of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 under this distribution is
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(x\right)\right\rangle =\left\langle x\right\rangle =0\times0.2+1\times0.5+2\times0.3=1.1.\]

\end_inset 

The expectation of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 under a distribution is known as the first moment of the distribution or
 its 
\emph on 
mean
\emph default 
.
 The mean can be thought of as the `centre of probability mass' of the distribut
ion.
 The expectation of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{2}$
\end_inset 

 is known as the second moment, it is normally used in combination with
 the mean to obtain the variance.
 
\layout Itemize


Variance
\layout Standard

The variance of the distribution is given by the difference between the
 second moment and the square of the mean.
 It is often denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sigma^{2}=\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle -\left\langle x\right\rangle ^{2}.\]

\end_inset 

It is always positive and it expresses the degree of variation around the
 mean value.
 Its square root is known as the standard deviation
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sigma=\sqrt{\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle -\left\langle x\right\rangle ^{2}},\]

\end_inset 

for our example above the variance is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
1.7-\left(1.1\right)^{2}=0.49,\]

\end_inset 

the standard deviation is therefore
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sqrt{1.7-\left(1.1\right)^{2}}=0.7.\]

\end_inset 



\layout ShortFoilhead


Distributions as Functions
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec:distributionAsFunction}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In the above example we defined a probability distribution by a simple table,
 making sure that the values summed to 1.
 A more compact way to represent a distribution is in terms of a function.
 As an example of this we will consider the Poisson distribution.
 

\layout ShortFoilhead


The Poisson Distribution
\layout Standard

The Poisson distribution was first published by 
\series bold 
Sim\i \'{e}
on Denis Poisson
\series default 
 (1781-1840) in 1837.
 It is defined over the space of all non-negative integers.
 Since this set is countably infinite in size it is impossible to write
 all the numbers down that would define the distribution.
 The Poisson distribution is therefore defined as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k|\mu\right)=\frac{\mu^{k}}{k!}\exp\left(-\mu\right).\label{eq:poissonDist}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 is any integer from 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset 

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 is a parameter of the distribution.
 In these notes we place 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 on the right of the conditioning bar to indicate that it is a parameter.
 Note that in 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \cite{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

 a slightly different notation is used.
 To work out the probability of a particular value, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

, for the a Poisson distribution with a particular parameter, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

, we simply substitute the parameter and the value 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:poissonDist}

\end_inset 

).
 For example, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=2$
\end_inset 

 we can start filling a table (Table 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:poissonTable}

\end_inset 

).
