Poisson Processes
\layout Standard

In Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec:distributionAsFunction}

\end_inset 

 we introduced probability distributions that were dependent on functions.
 In this Chapter we will introduce probabilistic processes.
 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Stochastic Processes
\layout Standard

A probabilistic process, also known as a random process or a stochastic
 process, can be viewed as a `random function'.
 A normal function maps a set of inputs to a point value.
 A probabilistic process maps a set of inputs to a probability distribution
 over values or a probability density function.
\layout Standard

Various systems are modelled with probabilistic processes.
 For example, the 
\emph on 
Wiener process
\emph default 
 is the process behind Brownian motion.
 Stock market data is often modelled as a stochastic process.
 For example the Black-Scholes formula assumes that the logartihm of the
 stock market data can be modelled as a Wiener Process.
\layout Standard

We will base our definition of a random process on that given by 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \cite{Chan:performance00}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Description

A\SpecialChar ~
random\SpecialChar ~
process may be defined as a 
\emph on 
random function
\emph default 
 which, for a particular value, is a random quantity.
 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Poisson Process
\layout Standard

We've already met the Poisson distribution, there is a simple process which
 is related to the Poisson distribution: the Poisson process.
\layout Standard

The Poisson process gives probabilities of a number of arrivals, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

, as a function of time, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, given a `rate', 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k|t,\lambda\right)=\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}e^{-\lambda t}.\label{eq:process}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

Note the similarity between (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:poissonDist}

\end_inset 

) and (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:process}

\end_inset 

).
 The process is simply the distribution with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 substituted with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda t$
\end_inset 

.
 The parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

 is now known as a rate, because the product 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda t$
\end_inset 

 gives the expected number of arrivals at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

By this substitution the mean of the Poisson process (the first moment of
 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

) is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\lambda t.\]

\end_inset 

Which is the expected number of arrivals at a given time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 if the rate of the process is 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 The Poisson process is a form of counting process where the counts are
 expected to increase linearly with time.
 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Properties
\layout Standard

In the next few lectures we will derive the Poisson process from some simple
 assumptions about how arrivals occur, for the moment we will highlight
 some important properties of the Poisson process.
\layout Itemize


Superposition of Poissons
\layout Standard

The first property we will consider is superposition of Poisson flows.
 It is a property of a Poisson process that if we combine 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

 independent
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed true

\layout Standard

Two variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

 are independent if their joint distributions are equal to the product of
 their marginal distributions 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x,y\right)=P\left(x\right)P\left(y\right)$
\end_inset 

.
\end_inset 

 processes with rates 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\dots,\lambda_{N}$
\end_inset 

 together the resulting process is also a Poisson process with a combined
 rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, equal to the sum of each of the input rates: 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=\sum_{n=1}^{N}\lambda_{n}$
\end_inset 

.
 This is shown pictorially in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:poissonSuperposition}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


