Example of Poisson Superposition

\layout ShortFoilhead


Question 
\layout Standard

There are three queues for registration of MSc students from different courses
 at the University of Sheffield.
 Students for the first course, Physics and Fine Art, arrive with a rate
 of 4 students per minute.
 Students for the second course, The Psychology of Sociology, arrive with
 a rate of 2 students per minute and students for the third course, Computer
 Science and Aromatherapy arrive with a rate of 5 students a minute.
\layout Standard

Assuming the student arrivals are independent and occur as a Poisson process
 what is the probability that after 15 minutes there have been 
\series bold 
a)
\series default 
 90 student arrivals? 
\series bold 
b) 
\series default 
180 arrivals?

\layout ShortFoilhead



\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard
%/*~
\backslash 
vspace{10cm}
\newline 

\end_inset 

Answer
\layout Standard

If the arrivals are indpendent and occur as a Poisson process, the rate
 of arrivals for all student is the sum of the rates for each course.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=4+2+5=11$
\end_inset 

.
 Now we simply evaluate (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:process}

\end_inset 

) with 
\begin_inset Formula $t=15$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=11$
\end_inset 

 and 
\series bold 
a) 
\series default 

\begin_inset Formula $k=90$
\end_inset 

 giving 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k=90|\lambda=11,t=15\right)=\frac{\left(11\times15\right)^{90}}{90!}\exp\left(-\left(11\times15\right)\right)=5.5\times10^{-11}.\]

\end_inset 

and 
\series bold 
b)
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k=90|\lambda=11,t=15\right)=\frac{\left(11\times15\right)^{180}}{180!}\exp\left(-\left(11\times15\right)\right)=?.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Now ask someone with a calculator to work this out.
 Factorial 180 will definitely not work.
 Explain that it needs to be computed as:
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k=90|\lambda=11,t=15\right)=\exp\left(180\times\ln\left(11\times15\right)-\ln\left(180!\right)-\left(11\times15\right)\right)=0.015.\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard
%~*/
\newline 

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

\SpecialChar ~

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize


Decomposition of Poisson Flow
\layout Standard

Just as Poisson processes can be combined to obtain a higher rate Poisson
 process, they can also be decomposed to obtain lower rate Poisson processes
 with lower rates.
 Assume we have one input channel where arrivals are occuring with a Poisson
 process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 This channel then splits into 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

 separate output channels.
 Each input arrival is then independently assigned to output channel 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(n\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 In this case the output channels then behave like Poisson processes with
 rates given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{n}=P\left(n\right)\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 This is shown in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:decompositionPoisson}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


