\layout Itemize


Independence of Arrivals
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:poissonIndependence}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Consider a single Poisson process, if we look within this process at two
 
\emph on 
disjoint
\emph default 
 time intervals (
\emph on 
i.e.

\emph default 
 periods of time that don't overlap).
 Assume we have 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset 

 arrivals in the first interval and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset 

 arrivals in the second interval.
 The distribution of arrivals in the two intervals are independent of each
 other, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k_{1},k_{2}\right)=P\left(k_{1}\right)P\left(k_{2}\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize


Similarity of Arrival Distributions for Identical Intervals
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:poissonSimilarities}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Consider a single Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, let the number of arrivals between time 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s+t$
\end_inset 

 be given by 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset 

.
 Now consider a second independent Poisson process with the same rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, let the number of arrivals between time 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r+t$
\end_inset 

 be given by 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 The distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset 

 are then equal, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k_{1}\right)=P\left(k_{2}\right).\]

\end_inset 

 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Interarrival Times and Poisson Processes
\layout Standard

The Poisson process is a simple model of arrivals that is widely used due
 to its analytical properties.
 We have already seen how a superpositions and decompositions of Poisson
 processes also lead to Poisson processes.
 Another attractive analytical property is the ease with which we can compute
 the 
\emph on 
interarrival time distribution
\emph default 
 for the Poisson process.
 
\layout Standard

The interarrival time distribution is the distribution of times between
 arrivals.
 Consider waiting for the next bus at a bus stop.
 If you just missed a bus how long will you have to wait for the next bus
 to arrive? If the buses arrive as a Poisson process, then it is straightforward
 to work out the distribution of these times.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/interArrivals.eps

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

For a Poisson process, this figure shows the times of the arrivals, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{n}$
\end_inset 

, and the interarrival times, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:poissonInterArrival}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:poissonInterArrival}

\end_inset 

 we show different arrival times, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1},\dots,T_{n}$
\end_inset 

 for the process.
 You can think of these at the arrival times of the buses.
 We are interested in computing the distribution of the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=T_{i}-T_{i-1}$
\end_inset 

: the times between bus arrivals.
 In the next section we will derive this PDF for the interarrival times
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

The interarrival times, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset 

, are continuous values, so they will be governed by a probability density
 function rather than a discrete probability distribution.
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize


Derivation of Interarrival Time Distribution
\layout Standard

Firstly we define the number of arrivals at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 to be given by the function 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 Next we note that the cumulative density function (CDF) of the interarrival
 time distribution will be given by 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right)$
\end_inset 

 (see Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:CDFs}

\end_inset 

 for a reminder on CDFs).
 We can re-write this probability as
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right)=1-P\left(X_{i}>t\right),\]

\end_inset 

so, rather than focussing on 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right)$
\end_inset 

, we can consider the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X_{i}>t\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 Now we rewrite
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed true

\layout Standard

The `
\begin_inset Formula $\equiv$
\end_inset 

' sign should be read as `is equivalent to'.
\end_inset 

 this condition in this distribution as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\left(X_{i}>t\right) & \equiv & \left(T_{i}-T_{i-1}>t\right)\nonumber \\
 & \equiv & \left(T_{i}>T_{i-1}+t\right),\label{eq:conditionInterarrival}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 

where we have simply used the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset 

 and rearranged the inequality.
 Let's take the time that 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i-1}$
\end_inset 

 occurs to be 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i-1}$
\end_inset 

.
 This lets us write the condition (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:conditionInterarrival}

\end_inset 

) as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(T_{i}>T_{i-1}+t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 This will be true if and only if the function 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

 does not change during the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(t_{i-1},t_{i-1}+t\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 We can express this mathematically as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left(X_{i}>t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\right)\equiv\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)=0\right).\]

\end_inset 

The right hand side of this statement expresses the condition that no arrivals
 occur between time 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i-1}=T_{i-1}$
\end_inset 

 and time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X_{i}>t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\right)=P\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)=0\right)\]

\end_inset 

now, because of the similarity of the arrival distributions for intervals
 of identical lengths (Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:poissonSimilarities}

\end_inset 

) we can write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)\right)=P\left(N\left(t\right)\right)\]

\end_inset 

which means that
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)=0\right)=P\left(N\left(t\right)=0\right)\]

\end_inset 

and substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)=k=0$
\end_inset 

 into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:process}

\end_inset 

) we find that the probability of their being no arrivals at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(N\left(t\right)=0\right)$
\end_inset 

, is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(N\left(t\right)=0\right)=\exp\left(-\lambda t\right)\]

\end_inset 

so
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X_{i}>t\right)=\exp\left(-\lambda t\right)\]

\end_inset 

and the CDF we are interested in is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right) & = & 1-P\left(X_{i}>t\right)\\
 & = & 1-\exp\left(-\lambda t\right).\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

Now to obtain the PDF we simply differentiate the CDF (Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:CDFs}

\end_inset 

) giving
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(X_{i}\right)=\lambda e^{-\lambda X_{i}}\]

\end_inset 

which is the interarrival time distribibution.
 The form of the distribution is recognised as an 
\emph on 
exponential distribution
\emph default 
.
 The parameter of the exponential, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, is the rate parameter from the Poisson process.
\layout Standard

In this section we have outlined the proof that the interarrival times for
 a Poisson process are distributed exponentially.
 Hereafter, we will use these formulations interchangeably, 
\emph on 
i.e.
 
\emph default 
when we state that the interarrival times for students registring at the
 Goodwin Sports centre is given by an exponential distribution with parameter
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2$
\end_inset 

 we are equivalently stating that these students are arriving according
 to a Poisson process with rate parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


