\layout ShortFoilhead


Consequences of Exponential Interarrival Times
\layout Standard

Let's assume that buses wait Waiting for a bus that arrives as a Poisson
 process ...
 you are more likely to arrive in a longer interval than a short one ...
 also it doesn't matter when you turn up, your expected wait never changes
 ..
 this is the memoryless property of the exponential.
\end_inset 



\layout ShortFoilhead


Properties of the Exponential Distribution
\layout Itemize


The Memoryless property
\layout Standard

We have already seen in our derivation of the exponential as interarrival
 time distribution that it is 
\emph on 
memoryless
\emph default 
.
 Formally speaking this means that for exponentially distributed variable
 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X>s+t|X>t\right)=P\left(X>s\right)\]

\end_inset 

or in English, the probability of the waiting time being greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $s+t$
\end_inset 

 given that the waiting time is already greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is the same as the probability of the waiting time being greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize


Mean and Variance
\layout Standard

Recall the exponential distribution 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p(x)=\lambda e^{-\lambda x}\]

\end_inset 

 The mean of an exponential can be found to be, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle x\right\rangle _{p(x)}=\frac{1}{\lambda}\]

\end_inset 

 and the variance is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle _{p(x)}-\left\langle x\right\rangle _{p(x)}^{2}=\frac{1}{\lambda^{2}}.\]

\end_inset 

 These are easily confirmed --- try doing them in your free time! 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Markov Chains and Markov Processes
\layout Standard

In the last Chapter we introduced the Poisson process.
 In this chapter we will show how the Poisson process in a special case
 of the more general class of processes known as Markov processes.
 We will start by considering Markov processes as they operate in 
\emph on 
discrete time
\emph default 
, otherwise known as 
\emph on 
Markov chains
\emph default 
.
 Both Markov processes and Markov chains exhibit the 
\emph on 
Markov property 
\emph default 
which is an assumption about how future values drawn from the process depend
 on past values.
 We will first discuss the Markov property in the context of Markov chains,
 then we will show how a discrete system can be converted to a continuous
 system.

\layout ShortFoilhead


Markov Chains
\layout Standard

Time is a continuous variable, but sometimes it is convenient, or natural,
 to discretise time.
 One example of a form of discretised time would be turns in a board game.
 As the turns progress this discretised form of time progresses.
\layout Standard

Consider a discrete representation of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0,1,2...$
\end_inset 

.
 We associate a state variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

, with this time such that we have values for 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0),\, N(1),\, N(2),\,...$
\end_inset 

.
 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Markov Property
\layout Standard

If this state variable exhibits the Markov property its value at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is dependent only on its value at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

.
 The common way of putting this property is that the future is conditional
 independent of the past.
 The conditioning being on the present.
 In other words, the state of the system in the future is 
\emph on 
not 
\emph default 
dependent on what happened in the past if we know the state at the present
 time.
\layout Standard

Let's consider a real-life example of a Markov chain, that of the board
 game of Monopoly.
\layout Itemize


Monopoly Board 
\layout Standard

There are forty squares on a Monopoly board.
 We assume that there is only one player.
 That player's state, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)$
\end_inset 

, will be the number of the square his/her counter is on 
\begin_inset Formula $+40$
\end_inset 

 for every time he/she has reached GO.
 For example if you have circled the board twice and are currently on GO
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)=80$
\end_inset 

.
 Or if you have circled the board once and are on King's Cross then 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)=45$
\end_inset 

.
 The time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is now clearly discrete and associated with the number of turns the player
 has had.
 
\layout Standard

In Monopoly you move your counter through rolling two six sided dice and
 adding them together.
 We will denote the result of this die roll at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)$
\end_inset 

.
 Notice that your state after turn 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)$
\end_inset 

 depends only on your die role, 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)$
\end_inset 

, and your state before turn 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t-1)$
\end_inset 

.
 We write this as: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
N(t)=N(t-1)+d(t).\]

\end_inset 

 This means that Monopoly exhibits the Markov property, as your future states
 are independent of your previous states given your current state.

\layout ShortFoilhead


State Diagrams
\layout Standard

A Markov chain can be represented by a state diagram just as finite state
 machines are often represented in this manner.
 One important difference is that Markov chains are not necessarily finite,
 they can have infinity states! A further difference is that the transitions
 for Markov chains are associated with probabilities (or in the case of
 Markov processes rates).
\layout Standard


