Steady State Behaviour
\layout Standard

Often we are interested in the steady state behaviour of a system, rather
 than the transient dynamics.
 We might ask the question what is the average network delay when the system
 has reached steady state? What do we mean by steady state? 
\layout Enumerate

A stable condition that does not change over time or in which change in
 one direction is continually balanced by change in another.
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard


\family typewriter 
http://www.dictionary.com
\family default 
.
\end_inset 


\layout Enumerate

A steady state is a dynamic equilibrium.
 
\layout Standard

A Markov process, under certain conditions, will reach a steady state distributi
on over 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

.
 Recall that a probabilistic process 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(k|t\right)$
\end_inset 

 is a `noisy function' of time.
 Some probabilistic processes will converge to a probability distribution
 when we look at them in the limit as time 
\begin_inset Formula $\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

.
 Not all will though, for example a Poisson 
\emph on 
won't
\emph default 
.
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}p\left(k|t\right)=p\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

the average value of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 at steady state is then given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle _{p\left(k\right)}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}p\left(k\right)k\]

\end_inset 

in what follows we will assume that this steady state average is equivalent
 to the time average in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

, where if the state of the system is given as a function of time as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

 the average number in the system as a function of time is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}\left(t\right)=\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}N\left(\tau\right)d\tau\]

\end_inset 

and the time average after in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\bar{N}\left(t\right).\]

\end_inset 

which we assume is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle k\right\rangle _{p\left(k\right)}\equiv\bar{N}$
\end_inset 

.
 See pg 156-157 in Bertsekas and Gallager for more details.
 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Little's Formula
\layout Standard

Applies for 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

/1 queues.
 
\layout Standard

Little's formula gives the relationship between the steady-state average
 size of a queue, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}$
\end_inset 

, the steady-state arrival rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, and the steady-state customer delay, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

Under steady state conditions, Little's formula states 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lambda\bar{T}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Nightclub Queue.
 You are driving past a nightclub, wondering how long you will have to wait
 to go in.
 You notice on Thursday nights the queue typically contains 20 people and
 that people arrive at a rate of 40 per hour.
 
\layout Standard

According to Little's formula, how long would you expect to have to wait
 to get in? 
\layout Standard

The waiting time is given by Little's formula.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}=20$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=40$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}=\frac{\bar{N}}{\lambda}=\frac{20}{40}=\frac{1}{2}\textrm{hour}$
\end_inset 

.
 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Graphical Proof
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/littlesFormula.eps

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Graphical `proof' of Little's formula.
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The area between the lines in the top figure can be expressed as both 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{t}N(\tau)d\tau$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{\alpha\left(t\right)}T_{i}$
\end_inset 

.
 Dividing both expressions by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, equating them, and taking the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 gives Little's formula.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}N(\tau)d\tau=\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{\alpha(t)}T_{i}=\frac{\alpha(t)}{t}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\alpha(t)}T_{i}}{\alpha(t)}\]

\end_inset 

 leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{t}=\lambda_{t}\bar{T}_{t}\]

\end_inset 

 We wish to consider the steady state behaviour.
 The system may not have steady state behaviour.
 If the steady state exists, we can seek it in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

, giving 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lambda\bar{T}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

This formula applies regardless of the arrival and service time distributions,
 it only relies on the system being in steady state.
 
\layout Standard

Little's formula can be used at the system level, as we have seen, or at
 a lower level.
 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Little's Formula for Queueing Time and Transmission Time 
\layout Standard

The total delay for packet switching systems can be divided into queueing
 time, 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset 

, and service time or transmission time, 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $T=X+W.$
\end_inset 

 Similarly the average delay may be divided.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\bar{X}+\bar{W}\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Standard

Both the queue itself and the transmission may be analysed independently
 using Little's formula.
 
\layout Itemize


The Queue
\layout Standard

Given 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, an arrival rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}_{Q}$
\end_inset 

, the average number of packets waiting in the queue, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}$
\end_inset 

, the average packet waiting time, Little's formula gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\lambda\bar{W}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize


The Transmission
\layout Standard

For 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

, the average transmission time, we can obtain the average number of packets
 under transmission, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\lambda\bar{X},\]

\end_inset 

 which is also known as the utilisation factor.
 It represents the fraction of time that the transmission line is in use.
 For a 
\begin_inset Formula $G/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queue it must be less than one, 
\emph on 
i.e.

\emph default 
 arrival rate must be less than 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\bar{X}}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

Note: time under transmission is often considered proportional to packet
 length.
 For packet length of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset 

 bits and bandwidth of 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset 

 bits per second transmission time is 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\frac{L}{C}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

You are at a queue in a bar, and notice that the bar tender spends 10% of
 her time cleaning between serving customers.
 If the typical number of people waiting to be served is 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset 

, what is the ratio of time spent waiting to being served? 
\layout Standard

Information says 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=0.9$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}_{Q}=3$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda=\frac{0.9}{\bar{X}}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
3=\lambda\bar{W}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\bar{W}}{\bar{X}}=3\frac{1}{3}$
\end_inset 

.
 

