Global Balance
\layout Standard

We seek a stationary distribution by looking for global balance equations.
 Global balance equations state that at equilibrium the frequency of transitions
 out of state 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 equals the frequency of transitions into 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

.
 (pg 260-262 Bertsekas and Gallager).
 
\layout Itemize


Discrete Time Markov Chain
\layout Standard

In its most general form a discrete time Markov process can be defined by
 a transition probability matrix
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{P}=\left[\begin{array}{ccccc}
P_{11} & \cdots & P_{i1} & \cdots & P_{N1}\\
\vdots & \ddots &  &  & \vdots\\
P_{1j} &  & P_{ij} &  & P_{Nj}\\
\vdots &  &  & \ddots & \vdots\\
P_{1N} & \cdots & P_{iN} & \cdots & P_{NN}\end{array}\right]\]

\end_inset 

if the steady state probability of being in state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(j\right)$
\end_inset 

 then the global balance equations state
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(j\right)\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}P_{ji}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}P\left(i\right)P_{ij}\]

\end_inset 

which in English says the probability of leaving state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

 is equal to the probability of arriving in state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize


Continuous Time Markov Chain
\layout Standard

In continuous time Markov chains we are interested in rates of transfer
 rather than probabilities if the rates of transfer between states are given
 by a matrix whose elements 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{ij}$
\end_inset 

 give the rate of transfer between state 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 and state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

, then the global balance equations give
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(j\right)\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}q_{ji}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}P\left(i\right)q_{ij}.\]

\end_inset 



\layout ShortFoilhead


Detailed Balance
\layout Standard

For the birth death processes we are considering transitions only occur
 between state 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k+1$
\end_inset 

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

.
 This leads us to a special case of the global balance equations known as
 the detailed balance equations which look at the flow between two states.
 For the discrete case
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k-1\right)P_{k-1,k}=P\left(k\right)P_{k,k-1}\]

\end_inset 

and for the continuous case 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k-1\right)q_{k-1,k}=P\left(k\right)q_{k,k-1}\]

\end_inset 

however in our notation the rate from 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 to state 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset 

 and the rate from 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{k-1}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/boundaryFlow.eps

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Flow rates at a Boundary.
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Thus at steady state, flow from 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 = 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{k-1}P\left(k-1\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 Flow from 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}P\left(k-1\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 For flow to be balanced we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda_{k-1}P\left(k-1\right)=\mu_{k}P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

 leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda_{k-1}}{\mu_{k}}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

 

\layout ShortFoilhead



\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

For the M/M/1 we assume that arrivals occur as a Poisson process, and service
 times are exponentialy distributed.
 This implies a birth death process with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{k}=\lambda_{k-1}=\lambda$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}=\mu_{k-1}=\mu$
\end_inset 

 giving 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\right) & = & \left(\frac{\lambda}{\mu}\right)^{k}\\
P\left(0\right) & = & \rho^{k}P\left(0\right),\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 where we have defined 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

, known as the utilisation factor.
 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Normalisation
\layout Standard

We can find 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

 by using the normalisation condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}P\left(k\right)=1$
\end_inset 

, therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \[
1=P\left(0\right)\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}\]

\end_inset 

 Giving 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\frac{1}{\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}}\]

\end_inset 

 The series in the denominator converges if 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho<1$
\end_inset 

 (when the birth rate is less than the death rate), 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}=\frac{1}{1-\rho},\]

\end_inset 

 meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left(1-\rho\right)\]

\end_inset 

 and allowing us to write down the 
\emph on 
steady state
\emph default 
 probabilities.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\rho^{k}\left(1-\rho\right)\]

\end_inset 



\layout ShortFoilhead


Design Issues
\layout Standard

For the design of networks we are interested in the average queue length
 (a design issue) and the average delay (a quality of service issue).
 
\layout Standard

We can use the steady state probabilities, in combination with Little's
 formula, to compute these quantities.
 
\layout Standard

Start by working out average number of customers in system (not the same
 as average queue length).
 
\layout LyX-Code


\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{N} & = & \left\langle k\right\rangle _{P\left(k\right)}\\
 & = & \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}kP\left(k\right)\\
 & = & \left(1-\rho\right)\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k\rho^{k}\\
 & = & \rho\left(1-\rho\right)\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k\rho^{k-1}\\
 & = & \rho\left(1-\rho\right)\frac{\partial\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}}{\partial\rho}\\
 & = & \rho\left(1-\rho\right)\frac{\partial}{\partial\rho}\left[\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}\right]\\
 & = & \rho\left(1-\rho\right)\frac{1}{\left(1-\rho\right)^{2}}\\
 & = & \frac{\rho}{1-\rho}\\
 & = & \frac{\lambda}{\mu-\lambda}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 



