\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 Queues with Vacations 
\layout Standard

Consider a server which, when it is free, attends to some other business,
 for example it could transmit control and record keeping traffic.
 
\layout Standard

These periods are sometimes known as vacations --- American for holidays.
 
\layout Itemize

If a new arrival arrives during a vacation it must wait until the vacation
 is over before being dealt with.
\layout Itemize

If a vacation ends and the server is idle it immediately goes on a new vacation.
 
\layout Standard

\SpecialChar ~

\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/vacationMG1.eps

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Schematic of 
\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queue
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We denote the length of these successive vacations to be 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{2}$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\dots$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1},$
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula $V_{2}$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\dots$
\end_inset 

 are i.i.d.
 random variables which are also independent of the customer times and the
 service times.
 
\layout Standard

For a Poisson arrival system with general service times we will look to
 compute the average waiting time for a system of this type.
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/vacationResidual.eps

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Computation of residual service time for system with vacations.
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

For this system 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}r\left(\tau\right)d\tau & = & \frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{M\left(t\right)}\frac{1}{2}X_{i}^{2}+\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{L\left(t\right)}\frac{1}{2}V_{i}^{2}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{M\left(t\right)}{t}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{M\left(t\right)}X_{i}^{2}}{M\left(t\right)}+\frac{L\left(t\right)}{t}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{L\left(t\right)}V_{i}^{2}}{L\left(t\right)}\label{eq:vacationResidual}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

On average the proportion of time available for utilisations is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1-\rho\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 If the average length of a vacation at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}_{t}$
\end_inset 

 then the number of vacations that will have arrived is 
\begin_inset Formula $L\left(t\right)=\frac{t\left(1-\rho\right)}{\bar{V}_{t}}$
\end_inset 

.
 Therefore if we take the limit of (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:vacationResidual}

\end_inset 

) as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 we find 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{R}=\frac{1}{2}\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle +\frac{\left(1-\rho\right)\left\langle V^{2}\right\rangle }{2\bar{V}}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

So given that the assumptions that we used to develop the formula 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{R}}{1-\rho}\]

\end_inset 

are still valid, we may write
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2\left(1-\rho\right)}+\frac{\left\langle V^{2}\right\rangle }{2\bar{V}}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

A bar has Poisson arrivals and one member of staff serving.
 When there are no customers at the bar, the staff member leaves the bar
 to check on the kitchen.
 Assuming these checks are exponentially distributed in length, with mean
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset 

 and that customer service times are exponentially distributed with mean
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 what is the average waiting time int he queue?
\layout Standard

Exponential standard deviation = 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

, mean
\begin_inset Formula $=\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

.
 So if mean is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset 

, standard deviation is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset 

 and variance is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}^{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 Thus second moment is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle V^{2}\right\rangle =2\bar{V}^{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 Similarly for second moment of service time 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =2\bar{X}^{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 Defining 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\bar{X}}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\bar{V}=\frac{\rho}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\bar{V}.\]

\end_inset 

Determinisitic vacations would have lead to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\bar{X}}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\frac{\bar{V}}{2}=\frac{\rho}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\frac{\bar{V}}{2}.\]

\end_inset 

 

\layout ShortFoilhead


Putting Queues Together
\layout Standard

Consider the simple system ivolving two transmission lines in tandem.
 Packet lengths are exponentially distributed, arrivals occur as a Poisson
 process.
\layout Standard

The first queue may be modeled as 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

.
 However the second queue cannot be.
 This is because a large packet will typically experience less delay at
 the second queue than a small one.
 
\layout Standard

This is a result of the second queue having a chance to clear while the
 large packet is under transmission.
\layout Standard

In Kleinrock's independence Approximation we simply assume that this correlation
 does not exist.
\layout Standard

Consider a general network with labelled nodes and links between these nodes.
\layout Standard

There are several packet streams in the network, each stream has a rate
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{s}$
\end_inset 

.
 We define 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{ij}\left(s\right)$
\end_inset 

 as the fraction of packets from stream 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset 

 that cross the link between 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

.
 The rate associated with a link between node 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

 is then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\lambda_{ij} & = & \sum_{s\textrm{ }}f_{ij}\left(s\right)x_{s}.\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

Kleinrock suggested that merging traffic streams on a transmission line
 has the effect of restoring independence of arrival and service times.
 The approach is to assume that each link can be treated as an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
 
\layout Standard

Good approximation for 
\layout Enumerate

arrivals which occur as a Possion process 
\layout Enumerate

exponentially distributed packets
\layout Enumerate

densely connected networks 
\layout Enumerate

moderate-heavy traffic.
\layout Standard

Given this assumption, from the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue we know that the average number at each link is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{ij}=\frac{\lambda_{ij}}{\mu_{ij}+\lambda_{ij}}\]

\end_inset 

thus the average number in the entire system is
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{N} & = & \sum_{ij}\bar{N}_{ij}\\
 & = & \sum_{ij}\frac{\lambda_{ij}}{\mu_{ij}+\lambda_{ij}}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

average delay at each link is given by Little's theorem
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}_{ij}=\frac{1}{\mu_{ij}+\lambda_{ij}}\]

\end_inset 


